Hashabiah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hashabiah) (d:name of several men, most of them Levites, among them the son of Kemuel who was officer over the tribe of Levi in the time of David) (r:13.6.45/16.10.11)
Hashabnah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hashabnah) (d:leader of the people who sealed the covenant in the time of Nehemiah) (r:16.10.25)
Hashabneiah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hashabneiah) (d:name of two men: the father of Hattush who repaired the wall of Jerusalem, and a Levite who led the people in praise) (r:16.3.10/16.9.5)
Hashbaddanah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hashbaddanah) (d:man who stood at the left of Ezra when he read the Law to the people) (r:16.8.4)
Hashem = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hashem) (d:Gizonite whose sons were among the mighty warriors of David) (r:13.11.34)
Hashmonah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:place) (w:Hashmonah) (d:place where the Israelites camped in the wilderness, between Mithkah and Moseroth) (r:4.33.29/4.33.30)
Hashub = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hasshub) (d:spelling of <Hasshub> in Nehemiah 11:15, a Levite, son of Azrikam and father of Shemaiah) (r:16.11.15) (q:draft)
Hashubah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hashubah) (d:son of Zerubbabel, a descendant of King David) (r:13.3.20)
Hashum = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hashum) (d:ancestor of a family that returned from the exile; also a man who stood beside Ezra at the reading of the Law, and a leader who sealed the covenant) (r:15.10.33/16.10.18)
Hasrah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hasrah) (d:keeper of the wardrobe, ancestor of Shallum, the husband of the prophet Huldah; called <Harhas> in 2 Kings 22:14) (r:14.34.22)
Hassena = (t:see) <Hassena-ah>
Hassena-ah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hassenaah) (d:man or family whose sons rebuilt the Fish Gate of Jerusalem in the time of Nehemiah) (q:draft)
Hassenaah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hassenaah) (d:man or family whose sons rebuilt the Fish Gate of Jerusalem in the time of Nehemiah) (r:16.3.3)
Hassenuah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hassenuah) (d:name of two men of Benjamin: an ancestor of Sallu, and the father of Judah who was over the New Quarter of Jerusalem) (r:13.9.7/16.11.9)
Hasshub = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hasshub) (d:name of several men, among them a Merarite Levite, father of Shemaiah, and the son of Pahath-Moab who repaired part of the wall of Jerusalem) (r:13.9.14/16.10.23)
Hassofereth = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hassophereth) (d:ancestor of a family of descendants of the servants of Solomon who returned from the exile) (r:15.2.55)
Hasufa = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hasupha) (d:ancestor of a family of temple servants who returned from the exile) (r:15.2.43/16.7.46)
hatang = (i:1) (t:n) (w:teeth/tooth) (d:<ha>, tooth, with <tang>, a single grain or piece: the teeth as separate pieces, called the grinders in Ecclesiastes 12) (s:hate) (q:draft)
hate = (i:1) (t:n) (w:teeth) (d:<ha>, tooth, with the plural <te>) (s:hatang) (b:ha) (q:draft)
Hathak = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hathak) (d:one of the eunuchs of King Xerxes, assigned to attend Queen Esther, who carried messages between her and Mordecai) (r:17.4.5/17.4.9) (q:draft)
hathat = (i:1) (t:adv) (w:quickly/swiftly) (d:<hat>, strong or fast, doubled, after a verb: with speed or force; {hong tung hathat} is come on quickly, and {ham hathat} is said of war horses crying out at the trumpet) (q:draft, low confidence)
Hathath = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hathath) (d:son of Othniel, in the family of Kenaz of the tribe of Judah) (r:13.4.13)
Hatifa = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hatipha) (d:ancestor of a family of temple servants who returned from the exile) (r:15.2.54/16.7.56)
hatin = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:strong/mighty/being strong) (d:<hat>, strong, with the linking suffix <in>: being strong; {a tha hatin} is being strong in body) (b:hat) (q:draft)
Hatita = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Hatita) (d:ancestor of a family of temple gatekeepers who returned from the exile) (r:15.2.42/16.7.45)
hatkik = (i:1) (t:v) (w:become strong again/regain strength) (d:<hat>, strong, with <kik>, again; {a tha hatkik} means his strength came back) (s:khauhkik) (b:hat) (q:draft)
