puknahte = (i:1) (t:n) (c:plant) (w:hyssop/hyssop plants) (d:<puknah>, hyssop, with the plural <te>: the hyssop plants that grow out of a wall) (b:puknah) (q:draft)
pukpah = (i:1) (t:v) (w:fall away at once/fall quickly) (d:<puk>, fall, with <pah>, at once: fall away immediately when trouble comes) (q:draft)
pukpih = (i:1) (t:v) (w:stumble with/fall with) (d:<puk>, fall, with <pih>, together with: fall or stumble along with someone) (b:puk) (q:draft)
puksak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:cause to fall/cause to stumble/offend) (d:<puk> with the causative <sak>: make someone fall, also make someone stumble in faith) (s:tuksak/khialhsak) (b:puk) (r:3.26.7) (q:draft)
puksakin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:throwing down/making fall) (d:<puksak>, cause to fall, with the linking ending <in>: throwing someone down and then doing the next action) (s:tuksakin) (b:puksak) (q:draft)
puksakkha = (t:see) <puksakkha-in>
puksakkha-in = (i:1) (t:v) (w:happen to make fall/cause to stumble) (d:<puksak> cause to fall with <kha>, which shows that the action may come about, and the linking ending <in>) (q:draft)
puksakna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:being struck down/knocking down) (d:<puksak> cause to fall with the noun suffix <na>; {puksakna thuak}, be struck down) (b:puksak) (q:draft)
puksakpa = (i:1) (t:n) (w:one who causes to stumble/offender) (d:<puksak>, cause to fall, with <pa> marking a man: the man through whom others are made to stumble) (b:puksak) (r:42.17.1) (q:draft)
puksakte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:those who cause to stumble/causes of sin) (d:<puksak> cause to fall with plural <te>) (b:puksak) (q:draft)
puksakzo = (i:1) (t:v) (w:be able to bring down/have thrown down) (d:<puksak> cause to fall, with <zo>, be able to or have done) (b:puksak) (q:draft)
puksi = (i:1) (t:v) (w:starve/die of hunger/perish) (d:<puk>, fall, with <si>, die: fall down dead; in the verses always of dying from hunger) (r:25.4.9) (q:draft)
puksih = (i:1) (t:v) (w:die of hunger/starve/perish) (d:form of <puksi> used before <sangin>, <ding> and similar words; {puksih ding} stands for famine in Job 5:22) (b:puksi) (q:draft)
puksisak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:starve/starve to death/cause to perish) (d:make someone fall dead, in the verses always by famine; <puksi> with the causative <sak>) (b:puksi) (q:draft)
puksite = (i:1) (t:n) (w:the dead/those who have perished) (d:<puksi> fall dead with the plural <te>; {kialin a puksite}, those who have died of famine) (b:puksi) (q:draft)
puksuk = (i:1) (t:v) (w:fall down/fall/slump down) (d:<puk> fall with <suk> downward: fall to the ground, also fall facedown before God) (b:puk) (q:draft)
puksukin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:bowing down/bending down) (d:<puksuk>, fall or bend downward, with the linking ending <in>; {a lu puksukin}, bowing his head) (s:kunsukin) (b:puksuk) (q:draft)
puksukpah = (i:1) (t:v) (w:fall down at once/drop down) (d:<puk> fall with <suk> down and <pah> at once) (q:draft)
pukta = (i:1) (t:v) (w:has fallen/is fallen) (d:<puk> fall with <ta>, which shows that the event has now taken place) (b:puk) (q:draft)
pukte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:those who fall/the fallen) (d:<puk>, fall, with plural <te>: people who fall or fall away; {sat pukte}, those who struck someone down) (b:puk) (q:draft)
pukthei = (i:1) (t:v) (w:can stumble/may fall) (d:<puk> stumble with <thei> can) (b:puk) (q:draft)
puktheih = (i:1) (t:v) (w:can stumble/may fall) (d:form of {puk thei}, be able to fall, used before <nading>; {a puktheih nading}, something that can make a person stumble) (b:puk) (q:draft)
puktheihna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:stumbling/cause of stumbling) (d:<puktheih> can stumble with the noun suffix <na>; {puktheihna ding suangtum}, a stone that makes people stumble) (b:puktheih) (q:draft)
pul = (i:1) (t:n) (w:plague/pestilence) (d:deadly disease that spreads among many people, also in {pul natna}) (s:pulnatna/pulpi) (q:draft)
Pul = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Pul) (d:king of Assyria, also called Tiglath-Pileser, who invaded Israel in the days of Menahem) (r:13.5.26) (q:draft)
Pul = (i:2) (t:n) (w:plague/pestilence) (d:<pul>, plague, with a capital at the start of a sentence; {pul natna}, a deadly epidemic disease) (s:pulnatna/pulpi) (b:pul) (q:draft)
pulak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:confess/declare/acknowledge/make known) (d:make something known openly: confess a sin or wrong, or declare the deeds of God among people) (s:genkhia/taangko) (a:im/seel) (r:18.32.17) (q:draft)
pulakin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:confessing/declaring) (d:connective form of <pulak>, confessing or making known openly, followed by another verb) (b:pulak) (r:16.9.3) (q:draft)
pulakkhia = (i:1) (t:v) (w:utter/speak out/declare) (d:<pulak>, make known, with <khia>, out: speak out, utter) (s:genkhia) (b:pulak) (q:draft)
pulaklak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:keep declaring/keep making known/reveal) (d:<pulak> make known, with the last syllable doubled for an action that goes on and on, parallel to <gengen>, keep speaking) (q:draft)
pulakna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:declaration/revelation/announcement) (d:<pulak> make known, with the noun suffix <na>; {pulakna bawl}, make a declaration) (b:pulak) (q:draft)
pulaknop = (i:1) (t:v) (w:want to declare/wish to confess) (d:<pulak> declare with <nop>, the form of <nuam> want used before a noun) (q:draft)
pulaksa = (i:1) (t:v) (w:confessed/declared) (d:<pulak> confess with <sa> already: what has been confessed or declared) (b:pulak) (q:draft)
pulaksak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:cause to declare/let mention/make proclaim) (d:<pulak>, declare, with the causative <sak>) (b:pulak) (q:draft)
pulepa = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ancestor/forefather) (d:{pu le pa}, grandfather and father: the forefathers of a people; {pulepa ngeina}, the tradition of the ancestors) (s:pupi/pusawn) (a:tuleta) (q:draft)
pulepate = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers/fathers) (d:{pu le pa}, grandfather and father, with plural <te>) (s:pupite/pute/pupa) (a:tuletate) (b:pulepa) (q:draft)
pulh = (i:1) (t:v) (w:fall off/drop/shed) (d:come off and fall, said of flowers, leaves, unripe fruit and hair) (r:3.13.40) (q:draft)
pulhin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:falling off/dropping) (d:<pulh> fall off, with the linking ending <in>, joining it to the next verb; of flowers and leaves) (b:pulh) (q:draft)
pulhkhia = (i:1) (t:v) (w:fall off/drop off/fall out) (d:<pulh> fall off with <khia> out, away: come loose and drop, as fruit or leaves from a tree) (b:pulh) (q:draft)
pulhkhiat = (i:1) (t:v) (w:drop off/fall off) (d:form of <pulhkhia> used before <bangin>) (b:pulhkhia) (q:draft)
pulhmang = (i:1) (t:v) (w:fall off completely/be shed/drop away) (d:<pulh> fall off with <mang>, away or gone: of blossoms, leaves or hair, fall and be lost) (q:draft)
pulhmangin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:fall off completely/be worn bare) (d:<pulhmang> with the linking ending <in>; here of hair worn away from the head) (b:pulhmang) (q:draft)
pulnatna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:plague/pestilence/disease) (d:deadly disease that spreads among many people) (s:pul/pulpi) (q:draft)
Pulnatna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:plague) (d:<pulnatna>, plague, written with a capital at the start of a sentence)
pulnatnate = (i:1) (t:n) (w:plagues/pestilences/diseases) (d:<pulnatna>, plague, with the plural <te>) (b:pulnatna) (q:draft)
pulpi = (i:1) (t:n) (w:plague/great pestilence) (d:<pul>, plague, with <pi>, great: a great plague) (s:pulnatna) (b:pul) (q:draft)
pum = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:round) (d:round like a ball; also used as a counting word for round things such as loaves and fruit) (s:bem/vial)
pum = (i:2) (t:n) (w:circle)
pumbukah = (i:1) (t:n) (w:in a furnace) (d:{pumbuk} furnace or forge with <ah> in) (q:draft)
pumguakin = (i:1) (t:adv) (w:naked/bare) (d:<pum>, body, with <guak>, bare, and the adverb ending <in>: with the body uncovered) (s:tangguakin) (q:draft)
pumkhat = (i:1) (t:n) (w:one body/one/unity) (d:<pum>, body, with <khat>, one: united as one; {pumkhat sakhat} is one flesh) (s:kikhatna) (r:49.5.31) (q:draft)
pumli = (i:1) (t:num) (w:four) (d:<pum>, counting word for round things such as fruit, with <li> four: four olives) (q:draft)
pumliin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:tremble all over/shudder) (d:<pum> body with <liin> tremble; written {pumliin’} before <gawp>) (s:pumlin) (q:draft)
pumlin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:tremble all over/shudder) (d:<pum> body with <lin> tremble; written {pumlin’} before <a>) (s:pumliin) (q:draft)
pumlingawp = (i:1) (t:v) (w:tremble violently/shudder all over) (d:<pumlin>, tremble all over, with <gawp>, severely) (s:linggawp/liinggawp) (b:pumlin) (q:draft)
pumnga = (i:1) (t:num) (w:five) (d:<pum>, counting word for round things such as fruit, with <nga> five: five olives) (q:draft)
pumnih = (i:1) (t:num) (w:two) (d:<pum>, counting word for round things such as fruit, with <nih> two: two olives) (q:draft)
pumpeeng = (i:1) (t:n) (c:plant) (w:reed/cane/rod) (d:reed; also a reed stalk used as a staff or a measuring rod) (s:pumpeng) (q:draft)
pumpeng = (i:1) (t:n) (c:plant) (w:reed/papyrus) (d:tall water plant growing in marshes) (s:pumpeeng) (q:draft)
pumpengte = (i:1) (t:n) (c:plant) (w:reeds) (d:<pumpeng>, reed, with the plural <te>) (s:phaikungte) (b:pumpeng) (q:draft)
pumpi = (i:1) (t:n) (w:body) (d:the whole body of a person; also the church as the body of Christ, of which the believers are the parts) (s:pumtak)
pumpi = (i:2) (t:n) (w:anatomy)
pumpite = (i:1) (t:n) (w:bodies) (d:<pumpi>, body, with the plural <te>; also of dead bodies) (s:luangte) (b:pumpi) (q:draft)
pumsung = (i:1) (t:n) (w:inside of the body/within) (d:<pum>, body, with <sung>, inside: the inside of a person, where desires are at work) (q:draft)
pumtak = (i:1) (t:n) (w:body) (d:the body itself, contrasted with the clothes that cover it) (s:pumpi) (q:draft)
pumtang = (i:1) (t:n) (w:whole body/body) (d:{a pumtang a phak mi}, a man leprous over his whole body) (s:pumpi) (q:draft, low confidence)
pumtangin = (i:1) (t:adv) (w:with the whole body/all over the body) (d:<pumtang>, the whole body, with the ending <in>: in or over the entire body) (b:pumtang) (q:draft)
pumthum = (i:1) (t:num) (w:three) (d:<pum>, counting word for round things such as fruit, with <thum> three: three olives) (q:draft)
pumtuam = (i:1) (t:v) (w:cover all over/envelop/cover) (d:<pum>, the whole body, with <tuam>, cover: cover someone completely, as shame covers a person) (q:draft)
pun = (i:1) (t:v) (w:increase/multiply/grow in number) (d:form of <pung> increase used before endings such as <na> and <bang>, written {pun’} with an apostrophe; {pun’na} is increase or offspring) (a:kiam) (q:draft)
pung = (i:1) (t:v) (w:increase/multiply/be fruitful/flourish) (d:grow in number or amount, said of people, herds and crops; also flourish) (s:khang) (a:kiam) (q:draft)
pungin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:increasing/multiplying/being fruitful) (d:<pung>, increase, with the linking ending <in>, joining it to the next verb) (s:khangin) (a:kiamin) (b:pung) (q:draft)
pungsak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:increase/multiply/make numerous) (d:cause to grow in number, <pung> with the causative <sak>) (s:phasak/khangsak) (a:kiamsak) (b:pung) (q:draft)
pungsakin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:increasing/multiplying/making numerous) (d:<pungsak>, cause to increase, with the linking ending <in> that joins it to the next verb) (s:phasakin/khangsakin) (b:pungsak) (q:draft)
pungzaw = (i:1) (t:v) (w:increase more/increase rather) (d:<pung>, increase, with <zaw>, more or rather: increase instead of growing fewer) (a:kiam) (b:pung) (q:draft)
punna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:increase/multiplying/growth in number) (d:<pun>, increase, with the noun suffix <na>: growth in number, also the offspring by which a family grows) (b:pun) (q:draft)
Punon = (i:1) (t:name) (c:place) (w:Punon) (d:place where the Israelites camped in the wilderness, between Zalmonah and Oboth) (r:4.33.42/4.33.43)
pupa = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers) (d:<pu> and <pa>, grandfather and father; <pupa ngeina>, the custom handed down from the ancestors, tradition) (s:pulepate/pupite) (q:draft)
pupi = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ancestor) (d:the first ancestor from whom a people or family descends) (s:pulepa/pusawn)
pupi = (i:2) (t:n) (w:forebear/forefather) (s:pusawn)
pupi = (i:3) (t:n) (w:root)
pupite = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers/patriarchs) (d:<pupi>, ancestor, with the plural <te>: the forefathers or patriarchs of a people) (s:pulepate/pute/pupa) (a:tuletate) (b:pupi) (q:draft)
Pur = (i:1) (t:n) (w:pur/lot) (d:the lot cast by Haman, from which the feast of Purim takes its name) (r:17.3.7) (q:draft)
Purah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Purah) (d:servant of Gideon, who went down with him to the camp of Midian) (r:7.7.10/7.7.11)
Purim = (i:1) (t:name) (w:Purim) (d:Jewish festival recalling the deliverance of the Jews in the days of Esther, named after the pur, the lot cast by Haman) (r:17.9.29/17.9.32)
pusawn = (i:1) (t:n) (w:forefather/ancestor) (d:<pu> grandfather with <sawn> onward: an ancestor further back than the grandfather; compare <tusawn>) (s:pupi) (q:draft)
pusuah = (i:1) (t:v) (w:go out/come out) (d:form of <pusuak> used before <ciangin> and <nop>) (a:sungtum/lut) (q:draft)
pusuahin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:bring out/take out) (d:<pusuah> with the linking ending <in>; here of bringing things out and handing them over) (b:pusuah) (q:draft, low confidence)
pusuahna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:going out/going/departure) (d:act of going out; paired with <sungtumna>, coming in: {na pusuahna na sungtumna}, your going out and coming in) (s:paikhiatna) (a:sungtumna/lutna) (b:pusuah) (q:draft)
pusuahpih = (i:1) (t:v) (w:bring out/lead out) (d:go out together with someone, taking them outside) (s:paikhiatpih) (a:lutpih/tumpih) (b:pusuah) (q:draft)
pusuahsak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:send out/make go out/put out) (d:<pusuah>, go out, with the causative <sak>: make people go out of a place) (s:paikhiasak) (a:lutsak) (b:pusuah) (q:draft)
pusuak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:go out/come out) (d:go or come out of a place; also of a voice, fire or words that come out; before <ciangin> and similar words the form is <pusuah>) (s:suakkhia) (a:lut/sungtum) (q:draft)
pusuakin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:going out/coming out) (d:<pusuak>, go out, with the linking ending <in>: going or coming out and then doing the next action) (a:lutin/sungtumin) (b:pusuak) (q:draft)
pusuakkhia = (i:1) (t:v) (w:come out/emerge) (d:<pusuak>, come out, with <khia>, out: come out from inside something) (s:suakkhia) (b:pusuak) (q:draft)
pusuaksak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:bring out/bring forth/produce) (d:<pusuak>, come out, with the causative <sak>: make come out, bring out of a store or out of the heart) (s:suahsak) (b:pusuak) (r:42.6.45) (q:draft)
pusuakte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:things that come out) (d:<pusuak>, come out, with the plural <te>: the things that come out) (s:paikhiate) (b:pusuak) (q:draft)
pusuakto = (i:1) (t:v) (w:come up out/come up) (d:<pusuak>, come out, with <to>, upward: come up out of something) (s:dawkto) (b:pusuak) (q:draft)
put = (i:1) (t:n) (w:look/appearance) (d:seen once after <mai> face, where the English has their faces are distorted with fear; meaning uncertain, not the word <pu> grandfather) (q:draft, low confidence)
Put = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Put) (d:son of Ham; also the people and land named after him, mentioned with Persia and Cush) (r:1.10.6)
pute = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers/fathers) (d:<pu>, grandfather, with the plural <te>: forefathers or ancestors, most often in the pair {pute pate}, grandfathers and fathers) (s:pupite/pulepate/pupa) (a:suante) (b:pu) (q:draft)
puteek = (i:1) (t:n) (w:old man/aged man/the aged) (d:<pu>, grandfather, with <teek>, old: a man of great age, also said of an aged father and of old people in general) (s:puteekpa/khanghampa) (a:tangval) (q:draft)
puteekpa = (i:1) (t:n) (w:old man/aged man) (d:<puteek>, old man, with <pa> marking a male person: the old man, one particular aged man) (s:puteek/khanghampa) (a:tangval) (b:puteek) (q:draft)
puteekte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:old men/the aged/elders) (d:<puteek>, old man, with the plural <te>: old men, the aged, the elders of a people) (s:putekte/khanghamte/teekte/teeksiate) (a:khangnote/tangvalnote) (b:puteek) (q:draft)
putekte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:old men/the aged) (d:<pu>, grandfather, with <tek>, old, and the plural <te>, a spelling of <puteekte>: old men, as in {pitek putekte}, old women and old men) (s:puteekte/khanghamte/teekte/teeksiate) (a:khangnote) (q:draft)
Puteoli = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Puteoli) (d:port city on the west coast of Italy where Paul landed on his way to Rome) (r:44.28.13)
Puth = (i:1) (t:name) (c:people) (w:Puthite) (d:clan of Kiriath Jearim in Judah, the Puthites) (r:13.2.53)
Putiel = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Putiel) (d:father-in-law of Eleazar son of Aaron and grandfather of Phinehas) (r:2.6.25)
Puvah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Puah) (d:son of Issachar, ancestor of the Puite clan) (r:1.46.13/4.26.23)
Ra = (t:see) <Ra-amses>
Ra-amiah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raamiah) (d:one of the leaders who returned from exile with Zerubbabel) (q:draft)
Ra-amses = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Rameses) (d:store city in Egypt built by the Israelites for Pharaoh) (q:draft)
Raamah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raamah) (d:son of Cush and father of Sheba and Dedan; also the trading people descended from him) (r:1.10.7)
Rabbah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:land/city) (w:Rabbah) (d:capital city of the Ammonites, captured by Joab and David; also a town in the hill country of Judah) (r:10.12.26/6.15.60)
Rabbath = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Rabbah) (d:Rabbah, the capital city of the Ammonites) (r:26.21.20)
Rabbi = (i:1) (t:n) (w:Rabbi/teacher/master) (d:Jewish title of respect for a teacher, used in speaking to Jesus as {Rabbi aw}; John explains it as <Sia>, teacher) (s:Sia) (r:40.23.8) (q:draft)
Rabbith = (i:1) (t:name) (c:place) (w:Rabbith) (d:town in the territory of Issachar) (r:6.19.20)
Rabboni = (i:1) (t:name) (w:Rabboni) (d:Aramaic title meaning my teacher, used by Mary Magdalene in speaking to the risen Jesus) (r:43.20.16)
Rachael = (i:1) (t:name) (c:female) (w:Rachel) (d:younger daughter of Laban, wife of Jacob and mother of Joseph and Benjamin) (r:1.29.6/1.35.19)
Rachel = (i:1) (t:name) (c:female) (w:Rachel) (d:younger daughter of Laban, wife of Jacob and mother of Joseph and Benjamin) (r:1.46.19)
Raddai = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raddai) (d:fifth son of Jesse, brother of David) (r:13.2.14)
Rafa = (t:see) <Rafa-te>
Rafa-te = (i:1) (t:name) (c:people) (w:Rapha) (d:<Rafa> with the plural <te>, the descendants of Rapha, fifth son of Benjamin) (r:13.8.2) (q:draft)
Rafah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raphah) (d:descendant of Saul through Jonathan, son of Binea) (r:13.8.37)
Rafu = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raphu) (d:man of Benjamin, father of Palti the spy) (r:4.13.9)
Rahab = (i:1) (t:name) (c:female) (w:Rahab) (d:woman of Jericho who hid the Israelite spies, mother of Boaz; also the name of a sea monster and a poetic name for Egypt) (r:6.2.1/6.6.17)
Raham = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raham) (d:descendant of Caleb, son of Shema and father of Jorkeam) (q:draft)
