Rommon = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Rimmon) (d:man of Beeroth, father of Rekab and Baanah who killed Ish-Bosheth) (r:10.4.9)
Rosh = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Rosh) (d:son of Benjamin) (r:1.46.21)
rubi = (i:1) (t:n) (c:stone) (w:ruby) (d:a red precious stone; loan word from English, used with <suangmanpha>, precious stone) (s:ruby) (q:draft)
ruby = (i:1) (t:n) (c:stone) (w:ruby) (d:a red precious stone; the English word kept in its English spelling, used with <suangmanpha>, precious stone) (s:rubi) (q:draft)
Rufas = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Rufus) (d:son of Simon of Cyrene and brother of Alexander; also a Christian in Rome greeted by Paul) (r:45.16.13/41.15.21)
ruhamah = (t:see) <Lo-ruhamah>
Rumah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Rumah) (d:town that was the home of Zebidah, mother of King Jehoiakim) (r:12.23.36) (q:draft)
Ruth = (i:1) (t:name) (c:female) (w:Ruth) (d:Moabite woman, daughter-in-law of Naomi, wife of Boaz and ancestor of David) (r:8.1.4/8.4.13)
sa = (i:2) (t:n) (w:flesh) (d:the flesh of the body of a person or an animal; {ci leh sa}, flesh, also stands for human nature and for one’s own kin) (s:satak/cilesa) (r:26.32.5)
sa = (i:3) (t:n) (w:animal/creature/game) (d:a wild animal, especially one that is hunted or eaten, game or prey; {sa beng} is hunt game and <gamsa> is a wild animal) (s:ganhing)
saal = (i:1) (t:n) (w:storehouse/barn/storeroom) (d:a building or room where grain and goods are stored; also of the storehouses from which God brings out the wind) (s:ansal/vankoihna) (q:draft)
saalte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:barns/storehouses) (d:<saal>, storehouse, with the plural <te>: buildings where grain and goods are stored) (s:ansalte/ansaalte/koihnate) (b:saal) (q:draft)
saam = (t:see) <saam-ek>
saam-ek = (i:1) (t:n) (w:dross/slag) (d:the waste that separates from a metal when it is melted and refined; the second part, <ek>, means dung or dross) (s:ek) (q:draft)
saan = (i:1) (t:v) (w:cast lots/cast/practise divination) (d:used after <ai>, <aai> or {lai-ai}, a lot: cast lots to decide or share out something, and also practise divination) (q:draft)
saan = (i:2) (t:v) (w:rise/mount up/rouse/rally) (d:rise up, as waves do; {tha saan} is rouse or rally one’s strength, and {hat saan lai} is the prime of life) (q:draft, low confidence)
saang = (i:1) (t:v) (w:accept/receive/take) (d:take what is handed over or offered; also accept something as true, and approve of it) (a:nial/nolh) (q:draft)
saangin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:receive/accept/take) (d:<saang>, receive, with the linking ending <in>: taking what is handed over, or accepting a plea, before a following verb) (b:saang) (q:draft)
saanin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:casting lots/casting) (d:<saan>, cast, with the linking ending <in>; {ai saanin} is casting lots) (b:saan) (q:draft)
saanna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:casting of lots/divination) (d:<saan>, cast, with the noun suffix <na>; {ai saanna} is the casting of lots, and also divination) (s:aisanna) (b:saan) (q:draft)
saante = (i:1) (t:n) (w:diviners/spiritists/soothsayers) (d:<saan>, cast, with the plural <te>; {ai saante} are those who cast lots to divine) (s:aisante) (b:saan) (q:draft)
sabakte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:pieces of meat/pieces) (d:<sa>, meat, with <bak>, piece, and the plural <te>: the cut pieces of a slaughtered animal) (q:draft)
sabakthani = (i:1) (t:phrase) (w:sabachthani) (d:Aramaic word kept in the cry of Jesus on the cross, {lama sabakthani}, why have you forsaken me) (r:40.27.46) (q:draft)
Sabakthani = (i:1) (t:phrase) (w:sabachthani) (d:Aramaic word kept in the cry of Jesus on the cross, {lama sabakthani}, why have you forsaken me) (b:sabakthani) (q:draft)
sabawh = (i:1) (t:v) (w:pounce on prey/seize prey) (d:<sa>, animal or prey, with <bawh>, pounce on: of a beast of prey, spring on its kill) (q:draft, low confidence)
sabbath = (i:1) (t:n) (w:Sabbath) (d:the seventh day of the week, the day of rest; {sabbath ni} is the Sabbath day and {sabbath tang} is keep the Sabbath) (s:Nipi/Nipini) (q:draft)
Sabbath = (i:1) (t:n) (w:Sabbath) (d:the weekly day of rest, written with a capital letter; {Sabbath ni} is the Sabbath day) (s:Nipi/Nipini) (b:sabbath) (q:draft)
sabbathte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:Sabbaths) (d:<sabbath> with the plural <te>; used with <tang>, keep, and with <palsat> or <siasak>, desecrate) (b:sabbath) (r:26.20.12) (q:draft)
sabbatth = (i:1) (t:n) (w:sabbath) (d:the word <sabbath>, here spelled with a second t, probably a misprint) (s:sabbath) (q:draft, low confidence)
sabeel = (i:1) (t:n) (w:pot of meat/meat pot/cooking pot) (d:<sa>, meat, with <beel>, pot: a pot in which meat is cooked) (q:draft)
sabeng = (i:1) (t:n) (w:hunter) (d:<sa>, animal, with <beng>, hunt: one who hunts game; compare <ngabeng>, fisherman, in the same verse) (s:sabengpa/bengpa) (q:draft)
sabengpa = (i:1) (t:n) (w:hunter) (d:a man who hunts animals; <sabeng> with <pa> marking a male person) (s:sabeng/bengpa) (b:sabeng) (q:draft)
sabengte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:hunters/pursuers) (d:<sabeng>, hunter, with the plural <te>: those who hunt or chase game) (b:sabeng) (q:draft)
sabet = (i:1) (t:n) (w:hunting/catching prey) (d:the hunting or catching of animals; {sabet siam} is skilled at hunting) (s:betna) (q:draft)
sabetna = (t:see) <sabetna-ah>
sabetna-ah = (i:1) (t:n) (w:in hunting/when hunting) (d:<sa>, game animal, with <bet>, hunt, the noun suffix <na> and <ah>, in: in the hunting of game) (q:draft, low confidence)
sabit = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:whole/single/entire) (d:{kep sabit khat}, one whole pearl: each gate made of a single pearl) (q:draft, low confidence)
Sabta = (i:1) (t:name) (c:people) (w:Sabta) (d:people descended from Sabta, a son of Cush; the name is written Sabtah in Genesis) (r:13.1.9) (q:draft)
Sabtah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sabtah) (d:son of Cush and grandson of Ham) (r:1.10.7)
Sabteka = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sabteca/Sabteka) (d:son of Cush and grandson of Ham) (r:1.10.7)
sabuaiah = (i:1) (t:n) (w:at the table/at table) (d:<sabuai>, table, with the place marker <ah>; {sabuaiah tu} is sit at table for a meal) (b:sabuai) (q:draft)
sabuaite = (i:1) (t:n) (w:tables) (d:<sabuai>, table, with the plural <te>) (b:sabuai) (q:draft)
sabuk = (i:1) (t:v) (w:lie in wait for prey/lurk) (d:seemingly <sa> prey with <buk> lie in ambush, of a young lion crouching in cover) (q:draft, low confidence)
Saddusi = (i:1) (t:name) (c:people) (w:Sadducee) (d:member of a Jewish religious party, linked with the chief priests, that denied the resurrection of the dead) (r:41.12.18/40.3.7)
Saddusite = (i:1) (t:name) (c:people) (w:Sadducees) (d:the Sadducees, a Jewish religious party that denied the resurrection, often named with the Pharisees) (b:Saddusi) (r:40.22.23/40.16.1)
Saf = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Saph) (d:descendant of Rapha, a Philistine giant killed by Sibbekai the Hushathite) (r:10.21.18) (q:draft)
Safeiros = (i:1) (t:name) (c:stone) (w:Safeiros/lapis lazuli/sapphire) (d:precious stone in the list of stones that adorned the king of Tyre, the sapphire or lapis lazuli) (r:26.28.13)
saffron = (i:1) (t:n) (c:plant) (w:saffron) (d:a plant whose flower gives a fragrant spice; loan word, here {saffron sing}, the saffron plant) (q:draft)
sagawh = (i:1) (t:n) (w:sacrifice/animal sacrifice) (d:<sa>, animal, with <gawh>, slaughter: the killing of an animal as an offering; used in {sagawh biakna}, sacrifice) (s:gangawh/kithoihna) (q:draft)
sagih = (i:1) (t:num) (w:seven) (d:the number seven; it stands after the noun it counts, and {a ni sagih ni} is the seventh day)
sagihin = (i:1) (t:v) (w:sevenfold) (d:<sagih>, seven, with the ending <in>; {a zah sagihin} is seven times as much) (b:sagih)
sagihin = (i:2) (t:v) (w:seven times) (d:<sagih>, seven, with the ending <in>; {a zah sagihin kithuk} is be avenged seven times over) (b:sagih)
sagihna = (i:1) (t:num) (w:seventh) (d:<sagih>, seven, with the ordinal ending <na>: the seventh in a series; {kha sagihna} is the seventh month) (b:sagih) (q:draft)
sagihte = (i:1) (t:num) (w:seven/the seven) (d:<sagih>, seven, with the plural marker <te>: the seven of a group already mentioned) (b:sagih) (r:66.1.20) (q:draft)
Sagihte = (i:1) (t:num) (w:the Seven) (d:<sagih>, seven, with the plural <te>, written with a capital: the seven men chosen in Jerusalem to serve at tables) (b:sagihte) (q:draft)
sagihvei = (i:1) (t:adv) (w:seven times) (d:<sagih>, seven, with <vei>, times: seven times) (q:draft)
sagihveina = (i:1) (t:num) (w:seventh time) (d:<sagihvei>, seven times, with <na>, making the ordinal: the seventh time) (b:sagihvei) (q:draft)
saguh = (i:1) (t:n) (w:bone/animal bone) (d:<sa>, animal or meat, with <guh>, bone: a bone of a carcass, as gnawed by beasts) (s:guh) (q:draft)
saguhte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:bones) (d:<saguh> with the plural <te>: the bones in a pot of meat) (s:guhte) (b:saguh) (q:draft)
sah = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:thick/dense) (d:thick, of a wall or of cloud and darkness; after a measure it says how thick a thing is, as {tong nga sah}, five cubits thick) (a:pa) (q:draft)
sahang = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:beast/wild beast) (d:a fierce wild animal; in Revelation, the beast that rises from the Abyss) (s:gamsa/sapi) (q:draft)
sahangte = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:wild beasts/beasts) (d:<sahang>, wild beast, with the plural <te>) (s:gamsate) (b:sahang) (q:draft)
sahawk = (t:todo) (q:draft, seen once in {sahawk khum kung} in a list of cargoes where the English has citron wood; the verse does not show what {sahawk} alone means)
sahin = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:thick) (d:<sah>, thick, with the linking ending <in>, joining it to the next description) (b:sah) (q:draft)
sahna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:thickness) (d:<sah>, thick, with the noun ending <na>: how thick a thing is, used in measurements of walls and vessels) (b:sah) (q:draft)
sahpi = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:thick/dense) (d:<sah>, thick, with the augmentative <pi>; said of cloud, darkness and a shield) (b:sah) (r:2.19.9) (q:draft)
sahpite = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:thick/dense) (d:<sahpi>, thick, with the plural <te>: said of thick clouds and thick darkness) (b:sahpi) (q:draft)
sai = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:elephant) (d:in the saying about the eye of a needle the Tedim text has {sai} where the English has camel) (q:draft)
sai = (i:2) (t:v) (w:be in charge of/manage/attend to) (d:look after a household or task; {kumpi-inn sung a sai}, the one in charge of the palace; {kigal sai}, opposite each other, is a separate expression) (q:draft, low confidence)
sai = (i:3) (t:n) (w:shop/stall) (d:in {anlum sai}, bread shop, where the English has the street of the bakers) (q:draft, low confidence)
saiha = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ivory) (d:elephant tusk, from <sai>, elephant, and <ha>, tooth; a costly material for thrones and houses) (q:draft)
saiha = (i:2) (t:n) (w:linen) (d:part of the word <saihadial>, linen, written here as two words: {saiha dial puan} is linen cloth) (s:saihadial) (q:draft, low confidence)
saihadial = (i:1) (t:n) (w:linen/fine linen) (d:linen, a fine cloth; mostly {saihadial puan}, a linen cloth or garment) (s:patpuan/puanpak) (q:draft)
saihate = (i:1) (t:n) (w:ivories/articles of ivory) (d:<saiha>, ivory, with the plural <te>: things made of ivory) (b:saiha) (q:draft)
Saikhar = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Sychar) (d:town in Samaria near Jacob’s well, where Jesus spoke with the Samaritan woman) (r:43.4.5)
Saili = (i:1) (t:n) (w:sling) (d:weapon for throwing stones; {saili kuang} is the pouch of the sling and <sailungtang> the sling stone) (s:suanglotna) (q:draft)
sailungtang = (i:1) (t:n) (w:sling stone/slingstone) (d:the stone shot from a {saili}, sling) (q:draft)
saingtho = (t:see) <siangtho>
Saingtho = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:holy) (d:misprint for <Siangtho> in {Kha Saingtho}, the Holy Spirit) (b:saingtho) (q:draft)
saipa = (i:1) (t:n) (w:administrator/steward/manager) (d:<sai>, be in charge of, with <pa>, a man: {inn sung saipa} is the man in charge of the palace or household) (b:sai) (q:draft)
saipi = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:elephant/big elephant) (d:<sai>, elephant, with <pi>, big; the Tedim text has it where the English has camel) (b:sai) (q:draft)
Saipras = (i:1) (t:name) (c:land) (w:Cyprus) (d:large island in the eastern Mediterranean Sea, home of Barnabas) (r:23.23.1/44.27.4)
Sairas = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Cyrus) (d:king of Persia who took Babylon and let the Jewish exiles return to rebuild the temple) (r:15.1.8/27.6.28)
Sairin = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Cyrene) (d:city in Libya on the north coast of Africa, home of Simon who carried the cross of Jesus) (r:44.2.10/40.27.32)
sak = (i:1) (t:part) (d:particle suffixed to form verb past tense)
sak = (i:2) (t:ppm) (d:postpositional marker indicating future action or state, equivalent in usage to the auxiliary verbs will and shall)
saka = (i:1) (t:part) (d:the causative <sak> with the linking <a> written as one word, placed after a verb: have something done and then go on, as in {bawl saka}, had it made and) (b:sak) (q:draft, low confidence)
Sakar = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sakar) (d:fourth son of Obed-Edom, a gatekeeper of the temple) (r:13.26.4)
Sakhar = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sakar) (d:the Hararite, father of Ahiam, one of the mighty warriors of David) (q:draft)
sakhat = (i:1) (t:n) (w:one flesh/one piece) (d:<sa>, flesh, with <khat>, one: a single body, said of husband and wife, and of parts made in one piece with something else) (q:draft)
sakhatin = (i:1) (t:adv) (w:of one piece/in one piece/as one piece) (d:<sakhat>, one piece, with the ending <in>: made in one piece with something else) (b:sakhat) (q:draft)
sakhawm = (i:1) (t:v) (w:sing together) (d:<sa>, sing, with <khawm>, together: sing together, said with <la>, song) (q:draft)
Sakher = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sakar) (d:the Hararite, father of Ahiam, one of the mighty warriors of David) (r:13.11.35)
sakhi = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:deer/gazelle/roe) (d:a small wild deer; the Bible uses it for deer and gazelles, often named together with <sanang>) (s:sazuk) (r:22.2.7) (q:draft)
Sakhia = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sakia) (d:a Benjamite, son of Shaharaim and head of a family) (r:13.8.10)
sakhino = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:fawn) (d:<sakhi> deer with <no>, young: the young of a deer) (b:sakhi) (q:draft)
sakhipi = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:doe/hind) (d:<sakhi> deer with <pi>, female: a female deer) (s:sangaipi) (a:sakhital) (b:sakhi) (q:draft)
sakhipite = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:does/hinds/deer) (d:<sakhipi>, doe, with the plural <te>; the feet of does are a picture of sure footing on high places) (b:sakhipi) (q:draft)
sakhital = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:deer/stag/hart) (d:<sakhi> deer with <tal>, the male of an animal; named among the clean animals that may be eaten) (a:sakhipi) (q:draft)
sakhitalno = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:young stag/young deer) (d:<sakhital>, stag, with <no>, young: a young stag, a picture of the beloved in the Song of Solomon) (b:sakhital) (q:draft)
sakhitalte = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:deer/stags/harts) (d:<sakhital>, stag, with the plural <te>) (b:sakhital) (q:draft)
sakhite = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:deer/gazelles) (d:plural of <sakhi>; the Bible uses it for deer, gazelles and does, and in Psalm 104 for the hyrax) (b:sakhi) (q:draft)
sakhituihup = (i:1) (t:n) (w:rainbow) (d:the rainbow, set in the clouds as the sign of the covenant of God with Noah) (q:draft)
saki = (i:1) (t:n) (w:horn/ram’s horn/trumpet) (d:<sa>, animal, with <ki>, horn: the horn of an animal, also a horn blown as a trumpet; in the visions of Zechariah horns stand for the powers of the nations) (s:ki/kii) (q:draft)
sakikh = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:badger) (d:a small animal of the rocks, named among the unclean animals because it chews the cud but has no divided hoof)
sakikh = (i:2) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:rock badger)
sakikh = (i:3) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:Rock hyrax)
sakikh = (i:4) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:dassie/Cape hyrax/rock rabbit/coney)
Sakikh = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:hyrax/rock badger/coney) (d:<sakikh> written with a capital at the start of a sentence) (b:sakikh) (q:draft)
sakikhte = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:hyraxes/rock badgers/coneys) (d:<sakikh>, hyrax, with the plural <te>) (b:sakikh) (q:draft)
sakin = (i:1) (t:part) (d:causative <sak> with the linking suffix <in>, placed after a verb: making or letting someone do it, and then going on to the next action) (b:sak) (r:23.3.17) (q:draft)
sakite = (i:1) (t:n) (w:horns/ram horns) (d:<saki>, a horn blown as an instrument, with the plural <te>) (s:kite) (b:saki) (q:draft)
sakkhawm = (i:1) (t:v) (w:feel together/share in) (d:<sak>, the form of <sa> feel or consider, with <khawm> together; {nuam sakkhawm}, be glad together with someone) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakkhit = (i:1) (t:v) (w:finish singing/have sung) (d:<sak>, the form of <sa> sing, with <khit> finished) (q:draft)
sakkik = (i:1) (t:part) (d:causative <sak> with <kik>, again or back, placed after a verb: make something be or stand again, restore) (b:sak) (q:draft)
Sakkuth = (i:1) (t:name) (w:Sakkuth) (d:a star god named together with Kaiwan in Amos, whose image Israel made and worshipped) (r:30.5.26)
saklam = (i:1) (t:n) (c:direction) (w:north) (d:the north, the opposite of <khanglam>, south; {saklam huih} is the north wind and {saklam gam} the land of the north) (s:leilu/leilulam) (a:khanglam) (q:draft)
saklamah = (i:1) (t:n) (c:direction) (w:in the north/to the north) (d:<saklam> north with <ah> in, at) (b:saklam) (q:draft)
sakluat = (i:1) (t:v) (w:find too/consider too) (d:<sak>, the form of <sa> feel or consider, with <luat> too much; {haksa a sakluat}, what he found too hard) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakna = (i:1) (t:n) (d:noun form of the causative <sak>: the act, place or time of making something be so, as in {lungdam sakna}, what pleases, and {kisian’ sakna mun}, a place of refining) (b:sak) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakna = (i:2) (t:n) (w:singing) (d:noun form of <sa>, sing: singing, in {la sakna}, the singing of songs) (s:lasakna) (q:draft)
sakna = (i:3) (t:n) (d:noun form of <sa>, feel or regard as: the feeling so named, as in {nuam sakna}, delight, what one finds pleasant) (q:draft, low confidence)
saknate = (i:1) (t:n) (w:songs/singing/musical instruments) (d:<sakna>, singing, with plural <te>; {la saknate}, the singing of songs, and with <tum>, play, the instruments of song) (b:sakna) (q:draft, low confidence)
saknate = (i:2) (t:n) (w:delights/pleasures) (d:in {nuam a saknate}, the things one finds pleasant, from <sa>, feel or regard as) (b:sakna) (q:draft)
saknung = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:upper) (d:seen once of a gate, {Benjamin Kongpi saknung}, where the English has the Upper Gate of Benjamin) (s:tungnung) (a:nuainung) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakol = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:horse) (d:the horse, in the Bible mostly an animal of war; {sakol tuangte} are horsemen and {sakol galkapte} cavalry) (q:draft)
Sakol = (i:1) (t:n) (w:horse) (d:<sakol> written with a capital, at the start of a sentence and in the name {Sakol Kongpi}, the Horse Gate) (b:sakol) (r:14.23.15) (q:draft)
sakolleeng = (i:1) (t:n) (w:chariot) (d:<sakol>, horse, and <leeng>, cart: a horse-drawn chariot) (q:draft)
sakolleengte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:chariots) (d:<sakolleeng>, chariot, with the plural <te>) (s:sakollengte/leengte) (b:sakolleeng) (q:draft)
sakollengte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:chariots) (d:<sakol>, horse, with <leng>, cart: horse-drawn war carts; <te> marks the plural) (s:sakolleengte/leengte) (q:draft)
sakolno = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:foal/colt) (d:<sakol> horse with <no>, young: a young horse) (b:sakol) (q:draft)
sakolpite = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:mares) (d:<sakol> horse with <pi>, female, and plural <te>) (b:sakol) (q:draft)
sakoltal = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:stallion/male horse) (d:<sakol> horse with <tal>, the male of an animal) (r:26.23.20) (q:draft)
sakolte = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:horses) (d:<sakol>, horse, with the plural <te>) (b:sakol) (q:draft)
sakpa = (i:1) (t:part) (d:causative <sak> with the male ending <pa>, placed after a verb: the one who causes it or makes it happen, he who made; also the one to whom it was done, as in {a nuntakkik sakpa}, the one who was raised) (b:sak) (q:draft)
sakpen = (i:1) (t:v) (w:think most/regard as most) (d:<sak>, form of <sa> regard or think to be, with <pen> most; {hoih na sakpen} is what seems best to you) (q:draft)
sakpente = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:hardest) (d:<sak> hard with <pen> most and the plural <te>: the hardest ones, said of rock) (q:draft, low confidence)
saksa = (i:1) (t:part) (d:the causative <sak> with <sa>, already done, after a verb: that has been made or caused to, as in {kisiang saksa}, refined) (b:sak) (q:draft, low confidence)
saksak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:harden/make hard) (d:<sak> hard, with the causative <sak>: used of hardening the heart) (s:khauhsak) (b:sak) (q:draft)
saksak = (i:2) (t:part) (d:the causative <sak> doubled after a verb: made someone do a thing, perhaps again and again) (q:draft, low confidence)
saksate = (t:see) <pian'saksate>
saktaak = (i:1) (t:v) (w:ought to regard as/be worth considering) (d:<sak>, form of <sa> regard or think to be, with <taak> worthy; {lamdang na saktaak} is what you ought to find astonishing) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakte = (i:1) (t:part) (d:the verb ending <sak>, cause or make, or <sak> from <sa>, consider, followed by the plural <te>: those who cause, those made, those considered; {pian’ sakte} are the created things) (b:sak) (r:23.29.7) (q:draft, low confidence)
saktheih = (i:1) (t:v) (w:can regard as/be able to consider) (d:<sak>, form of <sa>, regard or think to be, with <theih>, can, {mihoih a saktheih nading} is so that people may think them good) (q:draft, low confidence)
saktheihna = (i:1) (t:n) (d:being able to sing; <sak>, form of <sa> sing, with <theih> can and the noun suffix <na>; {la a saktheihna ding} is so that they may sing a song) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakuh = (i:1) (t:n) (c:animal) (w:owl) (d:a bird of waste places named together with <mu>; NIV has desert owl and screech owl) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakzaw = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:harder) (d:<sak>, hard, with <zaw>, more) (a:nipzaw) (q:draft)
sakzaw = (i:2) (t:part) (d:<sak>, cause, with <zaw>, more, placed after a verb: cause to be more so; {lungdam sakzaw} is please more) (b:sak) (q:draft, low confidence)
sakzawk = (i:1) (t:v) (w:regard more/feel more) (d:form of <sa>, feel or regard as, with <zaw>, more, used before <ding> and <bangin>, {lamdang sakzawk ding} is to be held in greater awe and {na a sakzawk} is feels more pain) (q:draft, low confidence)
sal = (i:1) (t:n) (w:captive/exile/prisoner/slave) (d:a person carried off in war, and the state of captivity: {sal-in man} is take captive, {sal-a om} is be in exile, {sal suak} is become a captive) (s:galmat) (q:draft)
Sal = (i:1) (t:n) (w:slave/captive) (d:<sal> written with a capital, at the start of a sentence and in {Sal Suakta}, freed slaves, the name of the Freedmen) (b:sal) (q:draft)
sala = (i:1) (t:adv) (w:as a captive/in captivity/in exile) (d:<sal>, captive, with the ending <a>: {sala om} is live as a captive, {sala mat} is take as a captive) (s:salin) (b:sal) (q:draft)
Salamis = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Salamis) (d:city on the east coast of Cyprus where Barnabas and Saul preached in the synagogues) (r:44.13.5)
saledi = (i:1) (t:n) (w:frankincense/incense) (d:frankincense, a fragrant resin burned as incense; usually in {saledi paknamtui}) (q:draft)
Salekah = (i:1) (t:name) (c:place) (w:Salekah) (d:town on the eastern border of Bashan, in the kingdom of Og) (r:13.5.11/6.13.11)
Salem = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Salem) (d:city of which Melchizedek was king, taken to be Jerusalem) (q:draft)
Salim = (i:1) (t:name) (c:city) (w:Salim) (d:place near Aenon, where John was baptizing) (r:43.3.23)
salin = (i:1) (t:adv) (w:captive/as a captive/into exile) (d:<sal>, captive, with the suffix <in>: {salin man} is take captive, {salin paipih} is carry into exile) (s:sala/galmatin) (b:sal) (q:draft)
Sallai = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sallai) (d:a Benjamite who settled in Jerusalem after the exile; also the name of a priestly family) (r:16.11.8)
Sallu = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Sallu) (d:name of two men: a Benjamite, son of Meshullam, who settled in Jerusalem after the exile, and a leader of the priests who returned with Zerubbabel) (r:13.9.7/16.12.7)
Salma = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Salma/Salmon) (d:name of two men: the father of Boaz, called Salmon in Ruth, and a descendant of Caleb, founder of Bethlehem) (r:13.2.11/13.2.51)
Salmon = (i:1) (t:name) (c:male) (w:Salmon) (d:son of Nahshon and father of Boaz, an ancestor of David) (r:8.4.21/8.4.20)
Salmone = (i:1) (t:name) (c:place) (w:Salmone) (d:cape at the eastern end of the island of Crete) (r:44.27.7)
Salome = (i:1) (t:name) (c:female) (w:Salome) (d:woman who followed Jesus, watched the crucifixion from a distance and came to the tomb with spices) (r:41.15.40/41.16.1)
salpihte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:fellow exiles/fellow captives) (d:<sal> captive with <pih>, together with, and plural <te>: those who are captives along with someone) (q:draft)
salsuahna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:slavery/bondage/captivity) (d:the state of being made a slave or captive; {salsuahna Izipt gam} is Egypt, the land of slavery, and {salsuahna panin} is from captivity) (s:saltanna) (a:suahtakna) (r:15.9.8) (q:draft)
Salsuahna = (i:1) (t:n) (w:captivity/slavery) (d:<salsuahna> written with a capital at the start of a sentence; {Salsuahna khuapi} is the city of captivity) (b:salsuahna) (q:draft)
salsuaksa = (i:1) (t:adj) (w:exiled/taken captive) (d:<sal> captive with <suak> become and <sa> marking a completed action: those who had been captives, the returned exiles) (q:draft)
salsuakte = (i:1) (t:n) (w:exiles/captives) (d:<sal> captive with <suak> become and plural <te>: those who had been taken into exile) (s:saltangte/salte) (q:draft)
Salt = (i:1) (t:name) (c:land) (w:Salt)
saltan = (i:1) (t:v) (w:go into captivity/be exiled) (d:be taken away as a captive; mostly as {saltan’na}, captivity or exile) (r:24.29.14) (q:draft)
saltang = (i:1) (t:v) (w:be in exile/be taken captive) (d:live as a captive in a foreign land; {saltang mi} is an exile) (r:24.29.4) (q:draft)
